Captures:     full capture-diff parity still pending (DECISIONS.md D2). Two targeted
              captures taken 2026-08-17 against the pinned image:
              1. CORS preflight + actual-response headers (OPTIONS/POST /users/ott,
                 GET /ping) — transcribed in src/middleware/cors.ts, asserted in
                 test/unit/cors.test.ts (D29);
              2. GET /users/details/v2 for a fresh free account, read twice —
                 established that museum's body is stable and its free-sub expiry
                 is creation + 100 calendar years, period "year"
                 (test/unit/subscription.test.ts, D30);
              3. auth-token transport across six private routes — `?token=` is
                 accepted everywhere, header wins over query, and the 401 bodies
                 are {"error":"missing token"} / {"error":"invalid token"}
                 (test/unit/auth-token.test.ts, D32).
              4. the whole TOTP two-factor flow — setup/enable/status/login/verify/
                 recover/remove/disable, including decoding museum's QR PNG to read
                 the otpauth URI parameters (test/unit/two-factor.test.ts +
                 test/unit/totp.test.ts, D36).
              5. SRP identity-guard + multipart part-count semantics, taken as a
                 DIFFERENTIAL run (same scenario list driven against museum and
                 against our server booted on :8581 with the hardcoded-OTT config,
                 statuses and bodies diffed): srpUserID collision on complete and
                 update, the first-time-only "SRP setup already complete" 400, the
                 same-id password change, cross-account setupID completion, and the
                 multipart/upload-urls count tables. 8 of 9 cases match exactly;
                 the 1 divergence (foreign setupID -> we 403, museum 200) is
                 deliberate and written up in D38/D39.
                 NOTE for future capture runs: museum rate-limits POST /users/ott
                 per IP and trips after ~10 accounts ({"error":"Rate limit
                 breached, try later"}), which silently truncates a long scenario
                 list. The limiter is in-memory — `docker restart
                 ente-oracle-museum` resets it while Postgres keeps the accounts.
                 Also: /users/srp/update REQUIRES updatedKeyAttr; omitting it is a
                 500 with an EMPTY body, which is easy to misread as a real finding.
              6. tokens/file-data semantics (D40, D41). Read museum's Postgres
                 schema directly (docker exec ente-oracle-postgres psql -U pguser
                 -d ente_db -c '\d tokens'), which is the fastest authoritative
                 answer for "does museum track X": the tokens table has NO expiry
                 column, so museum sessions never expire. Plus a differential of
                 PUT /files/video-data objectID shapes — museum answers 400 {} to
                 all of them, including a well-formed id on a nonexistent fileID.
                 LIMIT of this oracle: it cannot exercise any upload path from the
                 host. docker-compose.oracle.yml does not publish MinIO's port and
                 museum presigns for the in-network host `minio:3200`, so the
                 signature breaks if you rewrite the host. Anything needing a
                 committed file (file-data quota accounting, real vid_preview
                 commits) needs the client run INSIDE the compose network.
              Captures 1-2 predate the D31 compose fix and needed the OTT scraped
              from museum's stderr; captures 3-4 ran against the fixed compose.
